  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 329 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nPrimary forecast concerns are dense fog early this morning,\nanother MCS and severe weather threat for our far southern cwa\nthis afternoon, and the potential for a series of heavy rain\nproducing disturbances to increase flooding potential across\nparts of the area later tonight into the weekend. \n\nOutflow from Wednesdsay's MCS has pushed the effective frontal \nboundary well south of the area this morning. With modest mid- \nlevel height rises/subsidence in the wake of yesterday's \ndisturbance, relatively clear skies atop a residual moist \nboundary layer has led to fog development however, with calm \nwinds and mostly 0F temp-dew point spreads noted. Combination of\ncurrent GOES nighttime microphysics imagery and surface \nobservations depict fog quickly filling in south and west of the\nurban core of the Chicago metro, and expect this to continue \nwith several more hours until sunrise. Thus have issued a Dense \nFog Advisory for much of the area through 9 AM (except \nMcHenry/Lake and central/northern Cook counties for now) where \ncurrent obs and model trends suggest widespread dense fog is \nlikely. Visibilities will improve as fog gradually burns off a \nfew hours after sunrise this morning. \n\nAttention then turns to yet another convective complex \ncurrently tracking across eastern NE/SD, associated with the \nnext mid-level (convectively enhanced by the presence of an MCV)\nshort wave which is progged to transit the forecast area later \nthis afternoon. Current suite of CAM guidance is in reasonable \nagreement in this MCS approaching our western cwa toward midday,\nwith the strongest convective elements expected to track \nsoutheast along the northern periphery of the low-level \ninstability gradient into central IL (brushing our southwestern \ntier of cwa counties) into this afternoon. While some guidance \nhas indicated a gradual weakening of convection this far east \nlater today, diurnal warming should maintain instability along \nit's path and this may maintain primarily a damaging wind threat\nmainly along/south of about a Mendota IL to Fowler IN line \nearly this afternoon in addition to the potential for heavy \ndownpours. SPC's new Day 1 outlook has placed a slight (level 2 \nof 5) severe risk across this far south/southwest portion of our\nforecast area. Low level instability is expected to decrease \nmarkedly with further northward extent, limiting any severe \nweather threat farther north.\n\nConcern then increases for the potential for several periodic \nrounds of heavy rainfall and an attendant flood/flash flood \nthreat across portions of the area from tonight into at least \nSaturday. Guidance is in good agreement in strengthening an \nupper level high pressure ridge across the Gulf Coast northward \nacross the lower Mississippi and Ohio valleys during this \nperiod, setting up a period of persistent west-southwest \nwarm/moist advection flow around the northern edge of the ridge \ninto parts of IA and northern IL/IN. Model precipitable water \nvalues reach 2-2.3\" across the area as a series of short waves \ntop the ridge to our south. There is a consistent signal for \nportions of these areas to potentially receive several inches \n(3+) of rain, which on top of wet soils and elevated river/creek\nlevels in spots from recent heavy rains suggest an increasing \nconcern for flooding. The first round (aside from rainfall with \nthe afternoon MCS) would be tonight into early Friday morning, \nas a short wave currently emerging from the high plains of \nwestern CO/NE transits the region. Model QPF suggest a NW-SE \narc of potentially 2-plus inch rainfall generally from a bit \nsouth of roughly a Forreston IL to Valparaiso IN line overnight.\nWhile exact details remain a bit uncertain, elected to issue a \nFlood Watch for these areas from 7 PM tonight through 10 AM \nFriday. Depending on how rainfall lays out, the watch may need \nto be extended (or another issued) into the weekend for \nadditional periods of heavy rainfall.\n\nEventually, global models/ensembles shift the core of the Gulf \nCoast upper ridge westward late Saturday night into Sunday, \nwhich may allow the lingering frontal zone to our north to \nfinally push south of the area sometime late Sunday. Rain \nchances would likely diminish behind the front with mainly dry \nweather Monday/Tuesday. It does appear however, that another \nfront may push southeast across the area mid-week which may \nsupport some additional rain chances at that time. \n\nRatzer",
